text:
jordan: Heads up — we've started seeing heap growth in prod from the Java SSE client when the downstream consumer is slow. Symptoms: partial JSON frames queue up, Jackson parse errors from truncated input, and we end up buffering megabytes per connection. Current client is Netty + custom handler that accumulates lines in a StringBuilder then ObjectMapper.readTree(s). Anyone run into this and got quick mitigation ideas?

maria: Can you paste the minimal read/parse loop or a quick stacktrace? is this Netty HTTP client or HttpUrlConnection?

jordan: Netty HttpClient with our handler. Pseudo right now:
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
while (reader.hasNextLine()) {
  String line = reader.readLine(); // data: ...
  sb.append(line);
  if (line.equals("\n") || line.endsWith("}\")) {
    JsonNode n = mapper.readTree(sb.toString());
    queue.offer(n);
    sb.setLength(0);
  }
}
```
Problem is when downstream doesn't drain queue, loop keeps appending.

lee: Classic slow-consumer issue. Two quick wins: (1) Stop building whole strings — use Jackson streaming/non-blocking parser so you feed bytes and pull tokens. (2) Use Netty channel auto-read to pause the TCP read when your internal queue crosses a threshold. Example:
```java
if (queue.size() > MAX_QUEUE) {
  ctx.channel().config().setAutoRead(false); // pause inbound
} else {
  ctx.channel().config().setAutoRead(true); // resume
}
```
That prevents the socket from delivering more bytes until you can catch up.

kevin: Also add simple metrics so you can tune thresholds: sse.parse_queue_len, sse.inflight_bytes, sse.consumer_lag_seconds. Emit a backpressure event to the upper layers so they can degrade or drop non-critical streams. For Reactor/Reactive stacks you can also control demand with request(n) instead of pausing raw IO.

maria: On the partial JSON topic — rather than concat strings use Jackson's non-blocking parser: JsonFactory.createNonBlockingByteArrayParser() or the async byte-feeding API. Feed raw bytes and call parser.nextToken() in a loop; it will return tokens only when complete. This avoids transient truncated-object exceptions and removes the need to assemble full text before parsing.

jordan: Good call. Any suggested numbers for thresholds? we see bursts causing ~200-300KB per connection before we notice it.

lee: Start conservative: auto-read off at 256KB queued, backpressure signal to app at 512KB, and consider spill-to-disk or drop policy at 2MB. Also ensure netty recv buffer (ChannelOption.SO_RCVBUF) isn't huge, otherwise kernel will hold memory while you think you're paused.

deploy-bot: [alert] ingest-lag: sse.consumer_lag > 30s for service android-sse-client — https://internal/alerts/9351

jordan: I'll prototype: replace current StringBuilder path with jackson non-blocking feed, add queue cap + autoRead toggle + metrics. Will open PR and prototype flags for thresholds. pinging @maria @kevin for reviews. :thumbsup:
